tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Embedding similarity and format independence.}
SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space: unconditional pairwise $R^2$ is 1.3\%, rising to 7\% conditional on nonzero confusion; correlated aggregation amplifies this to batch-level $R^2$ of 18--25\% (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}).
The batch-level BCS--performance relationship holds across all RE settings---sentence-level ($R^2 = 0.22$) and document-level ($R^2 = 0.58$; IntraSub $\rho = 0.843$)---but not NER ($r \approx 0$, $n = 450$). A paired comparison of constrained vs.\ unconstrained decoding on 25 identical batch type sets confirms the BCS--accuracy relationship is format-independent (Pearson $r$: $-$0.45 vs.\ $-$0.44; Spearman $\rho$: $-$0.16 vs.\ $-$0.14; Fisher $z = -0.04$, $p = 0.97$). The task asymmetry (RE vs.\ NER) thus reflects error-mode structure, not output format: RE errors concentrate in type substitutions \cite{tam-2024-let-me-speak}, whereas NER errors scatter across missed entities, boundary misalignment, and type confusion \cite{schall-2025-hidden-cost}.

